The BEAM wasm kernel — one vertically-integrated runtime  #vision #wb-sjg3

This is the long-horizon moat thread that rides ALONGSIDE the wasmer migration (see
[[wasix-shell-migration]]). It is prioritized, not a someday-maybe: the migration is only "done" when
the two engines stop feeling like two engines.

## The thesis (say it plainly)

We did NOT write a wasm engine — claiming that is hype a knowledgeable person punctures in one question.
What we built (and keep building) is BIGGER:

> an **orchestration kernel on the BEAM** that turns interchangeable wasm engines into a single,
> supervised, capability-secure, dense, distributable compute fabric.

The engines — **wasmex** (component model) and **wasmer** (WASIX shell) — are commodity SUBSTRATE. The
moat is the kernel ABOVE them, made of exactly the things wasm fundamentally cannot do and the BEAM does
natively. It must FEEL like one system, not two, not three.

## What wasm fundamentally lacks — and the BEAM supplies (the whole basis)

These are not missing features; they are outside wasm's model entirely:

- a scheduler / preemption        → BEAM: millions of preemptively-scheduled processes
- a process / fault model         → BEAM: per-process isolation + supervision + "let it crash" restart
- inter-guest communication       → BEAM: message passing / mailboxes as universal IPC
- persistence / shared state      → BEAM: the tenant-partitioned Store + ETS
- distribution                    → BEAM: transparent multi-node clustering

So the move is NOT "run two runtimes." It is: wrap every wasm execution — a wasmex component OR a wasmer
process — as a supervised BEAM process, and let the BEAM be the scheduler/supervisor/router neither
engine has.

## The three primitives (the actual laurels) — wb-g5w3 / wb-qgpz / wb-y896

1. **Membrane** (wb-g5w3, FIRST brick = migration Phase 5) — ONE capability/effect bus over both lanes.
   A wasmex component's typed host import AND a wasmer shell command (work/agent/web/request) resolve to
   the SAME Elixir effect handlers (Nexus.Effects + the Dock). Two transports — a NIF call (wasmex,
   native) and a WASIX socket (wasmer) — identical semantics. Build Phase 5's host-cap socket bridge AS
   this transport, not a one-off. THIS is what makes it feel like one system.

2. **Cell** (wb-qgpz) — a wasm execution IS a supervised BEAM process. One uniform wrapper: spawn,
   mailbox, capability grant, resource meter, tenant binding, crash/restart — identical over wasmex
   (Nexus.Sandbox) and wasmer (Nexus.Wasmer). Reuse the Nexus.Worker.Supervisor patterns. Gives wasm
   what no engine has: preemptive cross-tenant fairness, supervision, backpressure, cluster-distribution.

3. **Fabric** (wb-y896) — cross-engine composition. Because Cells are BEAM processes on one Membrane, a
   wasmer bash stage can feed a typed wasmex component can feed another — one supervised compute graph
   with messaging + persistence between stages. NEITHER engine can do that alone. The novel compute model.

## The density moat — wb-s61c — "a billion things on 1GB"

The headline, and the part that excites people because it is a COST story, not a vibe:

- A wasm instance is **sub-MB** with no kernel and no OS-image floor — it is linear memory + the BEAM
  process wrapping it. A Linux container / Firecracker microVM is MBs-to-tens-of-MBs **plus a kernel**.
- CONSEQUENCE: on one **1GB Fly machine** we hold **thousands** of BEAM-scheduled wasm cells where
  container/microVM stacks fit **dozens** — work that elsewhere needs big servers, kernels, and per-seat
  subscriptions. One nexus scales because the BEAM scales (one-nexus-per-org, already canon).
- EXECUTE (don't just assert — back it with a published number):
  a. minimize per-invocation cost — precompiled/AOT modules, share one compiled module across instances,
     fast instantiation; the BEAM process is ~KB, the wasm instance is the real cost.
  b. lean LONG-LIVED cells (migration Phase 6) — because wasm sandboxes are so cheap, keep a per-agent
     wasmer session alive to amortize spawn + keep cwd/env/job; BEAM-supervised, idle/limit kill.
  c. MEASURE cells-per-GB and cold/warm spawn-ms on a real Fly machine and publish it. The claim only
     becomes a moat when it has a number on it.

## Two open R&D forks (honest, not hand-waved) — wb-p3fz

- **In-process wasmer vs subprocess.** Today the shell shells out to the wasmer binary (a subprocess).
  An in-process wasmer NIF (as wasmex does for wasmtime) would mean a Cell is a BEAM-managed instance
  with no subprocess — truest single-runtime feel + less spawn overhead. BUT two honest counterweights:
  (1) WASIX fork/threads in-process on the BEAM is gnarly; (2) the wasmer SUBPROCESS gives an OS-process
  boundary (seccomp/namespaces) an in-process NIF does NOT — so the subprocess is a defense-in-depth
  SECURITY feature, not pure overhead. Decide per-lane, with evidence.

- **BEAM as the encapsulation layer for wasm escape hatches.** The hard trust boundary for
  mutually-distrusting tenants stays the MACHINE (Fly Firecracker microVM / one-nexus-per-trust-domain),
  exactly as Nexus.Sandbox already documents — wasm SFI is innermost defense-in-depth. The kernel's job
  is to make the layering explicit and BEAM-mediated: machine = hard boundary, OS process (wasmer
  subprocess) = middle layer, wasm SFI = innermost. The BEAM mediates every capability across all three.

## Why credible, not greenfield fantasy — connect, don't invent

The pieces already exist; the moat is wiring them together:
- the **Dock** membrane (the invoke/effect seam) → becomes the Membrane.
- **Nexus.Effects** (open effect registry) → the one bus both lanes resolve into.
- **Nexus.Worker.Supervisor** (GenServer-as-.work, supervised, metered) → the Cell supervisor.
- literate **.work → one AST → dual-lane compile** → already routes to wasmex OR wasmer.

Full defensible stack: literate source → one AST → compiled to whichever substrate fits → executed as
supervised, capability-metered, BEAM-scheduled Cells over one effect Membrane, distributable across a
cluster, tenant-isolated, persistent. Nobody has "literate source → dual-substrate wasm →
BEAM-orchestrated effectful cells." That is the thing to put our name on, and the thing the Elixir + wasm
OSS communities get excited about.

## Sequencing (serves the migration AND the moat at once)

1. Finish migration Phase 1 — prove the wasmer lane in prod (the substrate works).
2. Build Phase 5 AS the Membrane's wasmer transport (wb-g5w3) — host caps compose; feels like one system.
3. Unify the Cell wrapper (wb-qgpz) over both lanes — supervision/metering/tenancy uniform.
4. Density pass (wb-s61c) + the in-process/subprocess spike (wb-p3fz) — publish cells-per-GB.
5. Fabric (wb-y896) — cross-engine graphs.

By the end, "two runtimes" has quietly become "one BEAM-scheduled fabric over two substrates."

## REALITY CHECK (2026-06-23) — blue-sky met the measurement. The honest verdict.

A dream→validate workflow (11 agents, grounded in the code) + a density benchmark settled what the moat
ACTUALLY is. Replacing the vision's optimism with measured truth:

- **ONLY real moat = the Membrane.** One capability bus where a wasmer-shell host-cap AND a wasmex-component
  host-import resolve through the SAME Elixir dispatcher with the same grant + tenant. "One security seam
  over two different wasm runtimes." That is the thing worth our name. Everything else is plumbing or research.
- **Density "thousands/billions per GB" is FALSE on the shipping lane.** Measured: a live shell Cell pins a
  real `wasmer` OS subprocess at **~67 MB RSS → ~15 cells/GB locally** (warm cache; a cold Fly box is worse,
  not better). Honest sellable line: **"hundreds of capability-secure multi-tenant cells per GB, fairly
  scheduled."** "Thousands" requires in-process module-sharing (research).
- **The split that the measurement points to (matches the AOT insight):** TWO tiers — (1) a per-invocation
  WARM POOL of AOT-precompiled, stateless, memory-shared boxes for one-shot commands (no cold start, the
  common case); (2) opt-in long-lived stateful Cells for workflows. Metric shifts from concurrent-count to
  **invocations/sec/GB**. Also: don't load python in every cell (it's likely most of the 67 MB).
- **Just-OTP plumbing (real work, NOT a moat), do honestly:** the Cell is textbook DynamicSupervisor+Registry
  AND today single-lane (wraps only wasmer, never wasmex — so "one API over two engines" is NOT built yet);
  "let it crash" is currently FALSE (`session.ex` does `{:stop, :normal}` on crash → no restart); the meter
  is just a run counter (no memory/fuel/bytes); Fabric does not exist; in-process wasmer-NIF + live-migration
  + deterministic-replay are blocked at the root (subprocess state, no serialize API, nondeterministic IO) —
  research bets, not roadmap.

### Build order (truth-per-effort — cheap claim-killers first)
1. **Density baseline on a real Fly box** (publish per-cell RSS + cells/GB for both lanes). Half a day. Done
   locally (~67 MB/cell); needs the deploy-target number to publish.
2. **Unify the grant path — THE moat deliverable.** One `Grant.resolve(grant, tenant)` feeding BOTH
   `sandbox.ex imports_for/2` (NIF lane) AND the Membrane allowlist (socket lane). Ship the GRANT-DRIFT test:
   one grant list → a component Cell + a shell Cell → an ungranted cap denied by the SAME code path on both.
   Today they're two security paths that can drift; folding them is the highest-value real work.
3. **Make Cell dual-lane** (route component→wasmex, shell→wasmer behind one Cell.call/stop).
4. **Real restart policy** (drop `{:stop,:normal}`; `:transient` + `{:DOWN,...}` to dependents).
5. **Normalized meter** (StoreLimits + timeout_ms + output bytes → one Cell.meter).
6. **Research spikes only, gated on a pass/fail test:** shared-module + memfd-CoW (the only path to real
   "thousands per GB"); AOT-precompiled warm pool for cold-start-free per-invocation runs.
